---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-sun-safety-kids`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Kid Sun Safety',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Kid Sun Safety', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Kid Sun Safety: Balancing Play, Sunscreen, and Skin Cancer Prevention"
  description="Childhood sunburns dramatically raise adult melanoma risk. How to balance kids' outdoor play, vitamin D synthesis, and skin protection — the family sun-smart routine."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-sun-safety-kids", label: "Kid Sun Safety" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Kid Sun Safety</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Childhood is the highest-leverage window for both vitamin D synthesis
      and lifelong skin cancer risk. Sunburns before age 20 more than double
      lifetime melanoma risk. Kids need outdoor play (many reasons — vision,
      circadian, movement, mood, vitamin D), and they need reliable sun
      protection.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The lifetime cancer risk</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      5+ sunburns before age 20 doubles melanoma risk. A single blistering
      childhood sunburn triples it. These are the highest-leverage prevention
      opportunities in dermatology — and they are entirely modifiable through
      parenting choices about hats, sunscreen, and midday shade.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Family sun-smart routine</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Sunscreen SPF 30+</strong> on exposed skin for extended outdoor time; reapply every 2 hours or after swimming.</li>
      <li><strong>Wide-brim hats and UPF clothing</strong> for the beach, hiking, extended outdoor sport.</li>
      <li><strong>Sunglasses</strong> — cumulative UV eye damage matters too.</li>
      <li><strong>Shade seeking</strong> during peak UV (10 AM-2 PM in summer).</li>
      <li><strong>Never seek sunburn</strong>, especially in children.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Doesn't sunscreen block vitamin D?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Real-world sunscreen use doesn't cause vitamin D deficiency in children
      (see our <a href="/vitamin-d-and-sunscreen" class="text-amber-700 underline">sunscreen page</a>).
      Kids get plenty of incidental sun through daily play — often more than
      their parents realize. The AAP position: protect skin, and supplement
      vitamin D if needed (400 IU/day standard).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical dosing</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Infants (breastfed): 400 IU/day cholecalciferol drops from birth (see <a href="/vitamin-d-for-infants" class="text-amber-700 underline">infants page</a>).</li>
      <li>Toddlers to age 12: 600-1,000 IU/day, especially at high latitudes in winter.</li>
      <li>Adolescents: 600-1,000 IU/day (see <a href="/vitamin-d-for-teens" class="text-amber-700 underline">teens page</a>).</li>
      <li>Sunscreen and hat use is orthogonal to supplementation — do both.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sunscreen for babies?</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      AAP guidance: under 6 months, avoid direct sun and use physical
      barriers (shade, clothing, hat, stroller cover). Sunscreen only if
      shade isn't possible; use small amounts of mineral (zinc oxide,
      titanium dioxide) on limited exposed areas. Over 6 months, standard
      broad-spectrum SPF 15+.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> The single most valuable long-term
      health investment for a child's skin is preventing sunburns while
      enabling outdoor play. Vitamin D supplementation handles the vitamin D
      side without compromising skin protection.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-sun-safety-kids" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
